1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:04,134
And speaking of emotions, what are your top tips

2
00:00:04,134 --> 00:00:06,000
on managing the emotions?

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,195
Like either you're super scared or you're super angry,

4
00:00:09,195 --> 00:00:13,550
or when you are prepping to speak up, choose courage.

5
00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:13,921
Yeah,

6
00:00:13,921 --> 00:00:15,752
so fear and anger, right?

7
00:00:15,752 --> 00:00:18,230
As we sort of all intuitively know, they have the opposite

8
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:19,404
action propensities, right?

9
00:00:19,404 --> 00:00:23,020
So fear will tend to make you sort of flee or freeze,

10
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:23,554
right?

11
00:00:23,554 --> 00:00:28,117
Fear is an avoidance emotion, whereas anger is an approach

12
00:00:28,117 --> 00:00:28,505
emotion.

13
00:00:28,505 --> 00:00:31,410
You know, anger makes you want to go toward the source.

14
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:33,556
So the advice has to be quite different,

15
00:00:33,556 --> 00:00:34,916
you know, with fear,

16
00:00:34,916 --> 00:00:38,193
you have to do things, frankly, often ahead of time

17
00:00:38,193 --> 00:00:39,701
over longer periods of time.

18
00:00:39,701 --> 00:00:42,285
You know, it can be about being in good physical shape,

19
00:00:42,285 --> 00:00:44,700
it can be about doing mindfulness yoga,

20
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,672
anything that sort of helps you change your base

21
00:00:47,672 --> 00:00:49,572
physiological response.

22
00:00:49,572 --> 00:00:53,846
People with high fear often find they have to also take specific

23
00:00:53,846 --> 00:00:56,220
steps like scripting out in advance

24
00:00:56,220 --> 00:00:59,874
things are gonna say. They may have to practice more and have

25
00:00:59,874 --> 00:01:03,469
people sort of shoot back at them so they can practice soft

26
00:01:03,469 --> 00:01:05,650
staying in the moment and not fleeing.

27
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:07,969
Most people don't physically run out of a room,

28
00:01:07,969 --> 00:01:10,204
but you'll see them just shut down and cave.

29
00:01:10,204 --> 00:01:14,355
And so you have to really practice tamping down the fear.

30
00:01:14,355 --> 00:01:14,951
Anger

31
00:01:14,951 --> 00:01:17,120
on the other hand, is in some respects useful

32
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:19,290
because if you get angry enough about something,

33
00:01:19,290 --> 00:01:22,668
you're actually likely to bring it up and say or do something

34
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:23,175
about it.

35
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:26,372
The problem with anger is you're likely to be quite unskillful,

36
00:01:26,372 --> 00:01:27,578
offensive, for example.

37
00:01:27,578 --> 00:01:31,099
And here I'll tell a story about myself.

38
00:01:31,099 --> 00:01:35,349
Most people, I think, in fact, almost everybody who knows me

39
00:01:35,349 --> 00:01:38,591
would say Jim has no problem choosing courage.

40
00:01:38,591 --> 00:01:43,323
But at times Jim has had a problem with displaying competent courage.

41
00:01:43,323 --> 00:01:46,770
And in most instances that would be because I let anger at,

42
00:01:46,770 --> 00:01:49,530
you know, injustices or problems or whatever get in the way.

43
00:01:49,530 --> 00:01:52,570
And so part of dealing with anger is what you do

44
00:01:52,570 --> 00:01:53,405
in the moment.

45
00:01:53,405 --> 00:01:57,408
Turns out these old adages like count to ten or take

46
00:01:57,408 --> 00:01:58,899
three deep breaths.

47
00:01:58,899 --> 00:02:03,543
These are actually quite useful because what they actually are

48
00:02:03,543 --> 00:02:07,577
doing is trying to engage your parasympathetic nervous

49
00:02:07,577 --> 00:02:09,100
system to calm down.

50
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:14,163
It's often a very useful tactic to try to teach yourself,

51
00:02:14,163 --> 00:02:16,315
to train yourself

52
00:02:16,315 --> 00:02:20,280
to accept in emergencies, not speak in that moment.

53
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:22,170
Let's schedule a follow up.

54
00:02:22,170 --> 00:02:26,263
Allow the moment to pass and then schedule after you've sort of

55
00:02:26,263 --> 00:02:28,508
gotten your emotions back together.

56
00:02:28,508 --> 00:02:31,839
And then, frankly, part of it is knowing who you

57
00:02:31,839 --> 00:02:35,596
are and using strategies, sometimes even technologies,

58
00:02:35,596 --> 00:02:36,384
to be your friends.

59
00:02:36,384 --> 00:02:39,240
So in my case, you know, it's a number of years ago

60
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:41,385
having made the classic mistake of,

61
00:02:41,385 --> 00:02:43,110
you know, firing off some emails

62
00:02:43,110 --> 00:02:46,889
when I was upset. I learned that you can actually

63
00:02:46,889 --> 00:02:52,006
set the outlook timer to basically hold all emails you send in the

64
00:02:52,006 --> 00:02:56,100
outbox for any designated number of minutes or hours.

65
00:02:56,100 --> 00:03:00,496
And so for quite some time, I set my outlook outgoing mail

66
00:03:00,496 --> 00:03:05,112
to hold for 60 minutes because I knew that if I basically didn't

67
00:03:05,112 --> 00:03:06,870
send emails for an hour,

68
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:12,012
there was a very high likelihood I would come down and revisit that

69
00:03:12,012 --> 00:03:16,064
email and have a chance to save it before I couldn't.

70
00:03:16,064 --> 00:03:20,439
So learning strategies for both lessening your anger and then sort

71
00:03:20,439 --> 00:03:23,400
of navigating around it are really important.

72
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,400


